{
  "summary": "The active_phases proposal from round 11 improves on terminal_stage but introduces its own problems. First, it conflates block selection with entry-point routing: declaring active_phases: ['impl'] means skipping planning and spec entirely, but the impl block (lines 1615-1616) calls run_implementing which consumes impl_spec and test_spec — with no spec-writing block having run, these fields are empty, producing garbage implementations. The proposal hand-waves this by saying items would be 'placed directly into the correct starting stage,' but that pushes the data-contract problem onto the human operator without validation. Second, the three-element active_phases enum creates a false sense of flexibility while actually being less expressive than terminal_stage for the most common case: a plan-only board. With terminal_stage: 'reviewing-plan', the semantics are obvious — stop after plan review. With active_phases: ['plan'], you need to know that 'plan' means the planning loop AND plan review, which is an abstraction leak from the code structure into user-facing config. Third, and most critically, both proposals ignore the interaction with the existing requires_human_approval field (line 1522-1527 in phases.py). When requires_human_approval is true and active_phases is ['plan'], a passed plan review routes to awaiting-human-approval — but then what? The pipeline resumes after human approval and moves to 'approved', which is the entry point for spec writing. Without an active_phases gate check at that exact transition, the pipeline falls through into spec writing despite the board config saying only planning should run. The check insertion points are fragile because they depend on understanding every stage transition in the waterfall, not just the block boundaries. The real recommendation: implement terminal_stage first as the minimal viable feature (it solves the stated use case with one config field and one check), add a stage-context field to items so final-human-approval knows what it's approving, and defer skip-early-phases to a follow-up that properly addresses data contracts and entry-point validation."
}
